{
  "$comment": "EVERY `padding` FOUND ON A STYLE IN A RENDERED CONFORMANCE SUBJECT, sorted, per subject. The pin asserts the distinct set is identical and no value occurs less often — not the multiset, because a Card draws a header box AND a body box where a Surface drew one, so the COUNT necessarily grows on a conversion that changed no spacing at all. RE-CAPTURED BY F-214, WHICH IS THE PIN WORKING RATHER THAN THE PIN BEING EDITED AROUND: ten sites wrapped their content in a padded View inside a Surface that already pads, rendering 24pt where every call site read 12. Removing those boxes makes values occur less often, which is exactly what this refuses, so the capture is part of that feature and its numbers are in progress.md. F-210 captured the first version BEFORE converting 24 Surfaces into Cards; that baseline has done its job and is in the F-210 commit. Asserted by apps/mobile/test/screens.test.tsx. WHAT IT DOES NOT SEE: rhythm, and overflow. Equal insets are not the same screen. RE-CAPTURED BY F-227, and by arithmetic rather than by hand: the scales became the mockups own (ADR-0103), so every inset moved through the same snap rule as every reference — 12 to 8, 20 to 16, 28 to 24, 40 to 32, 56 and 96 to 48, everything else where it was. The 1 is the Swatch keyline and is not spacing. This file is the EXPECTATION, so moving it by the rule and letting the render check it is the honest direction: a screen whose padding did not follow the rule fails here. F-239 ADDED ONE SUBJECT AND CAPTURED IT RATHER THAN COPYING ITS SIBLING: screens/Preferences (a display switch off) renders the engine card with one row off, which is the state the sweep never saw — every other subject draws all three switches in their drawn state. Its eight 16s are the engine Card header and body on top of the six this screen already had; the sibling entry above still pins the six it was captured with, and the rule that a value may occur MORE often is why that entry did not have to move. F-233 REMOVED THE SWATCH WELL: the mockups draw no padded well around a sample, so each Swatch lost its 8-dp well padding — 427 occurrences across 44 screens, taken out of this pin by exactly that count and nothing else. F-233 REMOVED THE STRIP'S WELL AND RING: each Strip lost its 8-dp well and its 1-dp ring inset, 4 of each across 4 screens, and nothing else moved. F-234 REMOVED ONE VALUE, AND IT WAS NOBODY'S: each Lens subject's 10 — one in each of the twelve, and nowhere else — was gorhom's default handle padding (its handle container pads 10 all round), which reached the tree through the library's own handle. The sheet now draws 03's handle itself, 5 dp below the sheet's top with no padded box (ADR-0118), so the 10 is gone and nothing else moved. F-236 RE-CAPTURED FOUR SUBJECTS, each an empty state that offers a way elsewhere and is now drawn as 27's card, padded md (16) on every side (ADR-0128 §2): OutfitBuilder (nothing wearable), Shopping (no wardrobe at all), Export (nothing to export) and Wardrobe (nothing in it). And the Lens awaiting a capture now draws 27's working card, padded md (16), where a skeleton stood. Home (first run) draws its two absences — no readings and an empty wardrobe — as 27's cards: each was an engine Card with a padded header and body (two 16s), and is now one card padded md (16), so two 16s left the subject and no value did. F-236 ADDED EIGHT SUBJECTS, each the witness a screen’s declared condition needs (ADR-0129 §3), recorded as first captured: nothing matches on the Atlas, the phrase found nothing on the Finder, a colour this corpus does not hold on Compare, ColourCard, ColourDetail, Contemporary and Wear, and a capture the Lens refuses. F-240 ADDED ONE SUBJECT, the colour-vision preview’s label above the tab bar (ADR-0131 §5), recorded as first captured. Nothing else moved. F-240’S REVIEW (S2) RE-CAPTURED THE SIX PREFERENCES SUBJECTS: section 2’s card holds the colour-vision pills padded sm (8), 15’s measured inset, where it had copied a header and body padded md; 8 is the one value added to each, and the 16s each subject had gained since the capture (sections 2 and 3 as cards) are recorded as they are now. F-237 ADDED FOUR SUBJECTS, each captured through this test’s own capture() rather than typed: 06’s bookmark off and on (no padded box) and the Atlas bookmarked-only and none-bookmarked (the Atlas’s own values, fewer entries).",
